Article 21

Assistance services as referred to in Article 19 letter a number 2 include:

a. accompanying victims during the examination and health recovery process; b. accompanying victims during the psychological recovery process;
c. accompanying victims during the medicolegal process;
d. accompanying victims during the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
e. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
f. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties without interest, including coverage by mass media;
g. coordinating with other assistants; and
h. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 34

(1) The Women and Children Protection Unit at the Village/Sub-district Level as referred to in Article 31 letter c, is a functional work unit that organizes integrated protection services and handling of cases of violence against children and women at the Village/Sub-district level.

(2) The Management of the Women and Children Protection Unit at the Village/Sub-district Level as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:

a. Village/Sub-district apparatus elements;
b. Village/Sub-district Non-commissioned Officer Coach (Babinsa) elements;
c. Community Non-commissioned Officer Coach (Babinmas) elements;
d. community leaders/religious leaders;
e. youth elements; and
f. Village/Sub-district PKK Driving Team elements
(3) The principal duties of the Women and Children Protection Unit at the Village/Sub-district Level as referred to in paragraph (1) are:
a. providing services in the context of protecting women and children;
b. receiving public complaints regarding cases of violence against women and children in the Village/Sub-district area;
c. facilitating women and children who are victims of violence in resolving their problems;
d. socializing to the community about the protection of women and children; and
e. providing assistance to victims or referring to the women and children protection task force at the Sub-district level.
(4) Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties and functions of the Women and Children Protection Unit at the Village/Sub-district Level as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be stipulated by Village Head/Sub-district Head's Decree.

Article 15

The Regional Government carries out activities for the implementation of protection of women and children from violence, which include:

a. prevention;
b. services;
c. social rehabilitation; and
d. empowerment.

Article 33

(1) The Women and Children Protection Task Force at the Sub-district Level as referred to in Article 31 letter b, is a functional work unit that organizes integrated protection services and handling of cases of violence against children and women at the Sub-district level.

(2) The Management of the Women and Children Protection Task Force at the Sub-district Level as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:

a. sub-district apparatus elements;
b. agencies/institutions at the Sub-district level;
c. Sector Police (Polsek) elements;
d. Military District Command (Koramil) elements;
e. community leaders/religious leaders;
f. youth elements; and
g. Sub-district PKK Driving Team elements.
(3) The principal duties of the Women and Children Protection Task Force at the Sub-district Level as referred to in paragraph (1) are:
a. providing services in the context of protecting women and children;
b. receiving public complaints regarding cases of violence against women and children in the Sub-district and Village/Sub-district areas;
c. facilitating women and children who are victims of violence in resolving their problems;
d. socializing to the community about the protection of women and children; and
e. providing assistance to victims or referring to P2TP2A, Sector Police (Polsek)/Regional Police (Polres), hospitals or other necessary institutions.
(4) Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties, and functions of the Women and Children Protection Task Force at the Sub-district Level as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be stipulated by Sub-district Head's Decree.

Article 8

Women and children who are victims of violence receive the following rights: a. the right to be respected for their dignity as human beings;

b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims;

c. the right to determine their own decisions;

d. the right to obtain information;

e. the right to confidentiality;

f. the right to compensation;

g. the right to social rehabilitation;

h. the right to complaint handling;

i. the right to assistance;

j. the right to obtain visum et repertum free of charge; and

k. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process.

Article 32

(1) P2TP2A as referred to in Article 31 letter a, is a community-based women and children protection service facility that functions as an integrated service center in providing protection to women and children from violence.

(2) The membership composition of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) among others consists of:

a. elements of the Regional Government;
b. academics;
c. legal experts;
d. psychologists;
e. psychiatrists;
f. religious leaders; and
g. community elements.
(3) The principal duties of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) are: a. providing protection to women and children; and b. improving the quality of life of women and children.
(4) Further provisions regarding the establishment of P2TP2A shall be stipulated by Regent's Decree.

Article 25

(1) Forms of services as referred to in Article 19, are implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the Government and implemented by the Regency Regional Apparatus that has tasks and functions in the fields of:

a. women's empowerment and child protection;
b. social affairs;
c. labor;
d. health;
e. education;
f. security and order; and
g. mental and spiritual.
(2) In carrying out the tasks and functions as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government cooperates with the Government, Provincial Government, other Regency/City Governments, and the community.

Article 16

(1) Prevention as referred to in Article 15 letter a, is implemented by way of:

a. forming a network in efforts to prevent violence;
b. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
c. establishing a violence prevention system;
d. conducting socialization about laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children victims of violence; and
e. providing knowledge about prevention and mechanisms for addressing violence against women and children.
(2) Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented in an integrated manner by the Regency Regional Apparatus and related agencies that have main tasks and functions in the fields of:
a. women's empowerment and child protection;
b. education;
c. health;
d. social affairs;
e. labor;
f. youth and sports; and
g. mental and spiritual.
(3) In addition to being implemented by the Regency Regional Apparatus and related agencies as referred to in paragraph (2), prevention is also implemented by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. the community, NGOs, community organizations, social organizations and religious organizations; and
c. the business community.

